{
 "title": "Mining Financial Data using Mixtures of Mirrored Weibull Distributions - Replication Spec",
 "hypothesis": "MMW provides more accurate VaR estimates/forecasts than GMM and tMM for non-normal stock returns",
 "data_sources": [
  "S&P500 adjusted closing prices for BRK, WMT, CVS"
 ],
 "sample": "3 stocks: BRK monthly log price ratio returns, WMT monthly log price ratio returns, CVS* daily log price ratio returns; calculated on adjusted closing prices as log price ratio. No filters stated. Sample sizes (n) not printed.",
 "period_start": "June 1996",
 "period_end": "Jun 2024",
 "method": [
  "Step 1 - Returns: x = log price ratio of adjusted closing prices as defined in Section 3.1 (Table 1 notes daily* for CVS).",
  "Step 2 - Mirror transform: y_j = c - x_j where c = ceil(|x_(n)|), x_(n)=largest observation; f_MW(x;mu,sigma) = sigma/mu * ((c-x)/mu)^(sigma-1) * exp(-((c-x)/mu)^sigma) for x <= c.",
  "Step 3 - Model: f(x;Psi)=sum_{i=1}^g pi_i f_MW(x;mu_i,sigma_i), sum pi_i=1, m=3g-1 free parameters.",
  "Step 4 - Estimation: ML via EM. Init: k-means (or hierarchical/random) on y, z_{ij}^{(0)} membership, pi_i^{(0)}=sum_j z_{ij}^{(0)}, mu_i^{(0)}= ybar_i / Gamma(1+1/sigma_i^{(0)}), sigma_i^{(0)} solves s_i^2/ybar_i^2 = [Gamma(1+2/sigma)-Gamma(1+1/sigma)^2]/Gamma(1+1/sigma)^2. E-step: z_{ij}^{(k)}= pi_i^{(k)} f_W(y_j;mu_i^{(k)},sigma_i^{(k)}) / sum_h pi_h^{(k)} f_W(y_j;...). M-step: pi_i^{(k)}=1/n sum_j z_{ij}^{(k)}; sigma_i^{(k)}=[sum_j z_{ij}^{(k)} y_j^{mu_i^{(k)}} / sum_j z_{ij}^{(k)}]^{1/mu_i^{(k)}}; mu_i^{(k)} solves [sum z y^{mu} log y / sum z y^{mu} - sum z log y / sum z]^{-1} (numeric; Cohen-Whitten approx allowed). Log-likelihood l^{(k)} as Eq logL.",
  "Step 5 - Stopping: k>=k_max=500 or |l^{(k)}-l^{(k-1)}|<epsilon=1e-6.",
  "Step 6 - Choose g: run g=1,2,3,4, select min BIC = m log n -2 log hat l; most frequent g=2.",
  "Step 7 - VaR: Definition VaR_alpha = F_X^{-1}(1-alpha). Estimation by simulation from fitted MMW/GMM/tMM (cdf closed form available). Levels alpha=5% and alpha=1% (1% for WMT,CVS). Empirical VaR is historical quantile.",
  "Step 8 - Forecast: One-day ahead rolling window 250 days (~1yr) for CVS, advance 1 day until last date.",
  "Step 9 - Assessment: Kupiec POF/unconditional coverage/backtest LR_POF and Christoffersen independence LR_IND with p-values; pass/fail as printed; MSE and failure rate for CVS forecasts."
 ],
